# Exploratory Data Analysis: DecodeLabs Project 2

Exploratory analysis of 1,200 customer orders (Jan 2023 to Jun 2025),
using the cleaned dataset from Project 1.

## Questions answered
- What does a typical order look like?
- Are there outliers, and are they errors or real?
- Which products perform best?
- How do sales change over time?
- How many orders are actually delivered?

## Key findings
- Mean order value is 1,053.97 but the median is 823.62 (right-skewed data)
- 8 high-value outliers: genuine 5-unit orders, kept in the data
- UnitPrice (r = 0.72) affects order value slightly more than Quantity (r = 0.62)
- Chair and Printer earn the most (about 195.6k each); Phone earns the least
- Only about 19% of orders were delivered; about 41% were cancelled or returned
- First-half revenue fell about 10% each year (2023 to 2025)

## Methods
Descriptive statistics, IQR outlier detection, correlation analysis,
group comparisons.

## Tools
Google Colab, Python (pandas), Google Sheets

## Files
- EDA_notebook.ipynb: the analysis code
- EDA_summary.pdf: executive summary
- Decode labs P1 - Cleaned Data set.xlsx: the cleaned dataset

## Limitations
Revenue includes cancelled and returned orders, so real revenue is likely
lower. TotalPrice is before coupon discounts. Patterns shown here are not
proof of cause.
